            <ArticleTitle>Incidence of back pain after lumbar epidural anaesthesia for non-obstetric surgery--a preliminary report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>241-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This prospective survey attempt to study the incidence of post-operative back pain after lumbar epidural anesthesia for non-obstetric patterns and the correlation of this symptom with various contributing factors. One hundred and five patients who were given lumbar epidural anaesthesia as the sole anaesthesia for non-obstetric surgery were studied. The choice of equipment, number of attempts at giving the injection, duration of surgery and position of patient during surgery were documented. One week post-operatively, the patients were asked whether they recalled any back pain. The nature, duration and severity of the back pain was documented. Statistical analysis was achieved by using Chi-squared test. Twenty-eight patients recalled &quot;injection site tenderness&quot; post-operatively. The pain was mild to moderate in severity and lasted up to 4 days. None of the studied patients had post-operative &quot;backache&quot;. The pain showed no significant correlation with needle size, technique of injection, use of epidural catheter, patient's position during surgery, duration and number of attempts made during epidural injection.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
